course: dictionary
lesson: 9044
title: One word, two jobs: sled
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A sled is a small vehicle that slides over snow, used for fun on hills. The same word can also be an action: to sled means to ride a sled. The sentence tells you which job the word is doing.
explain: Look at «Stop sledding when it gets dark, because you cannot see obstacles.» Sledding is something you do, and the words snow and hill show it happens outdoors in winter. Obstacles are things in your way, so the sentence warns you that you cannot see what is in your path in the dark.
explain: In the UK the same vehicle is called a sledge, and in Australia people sometimes say toboggan. All three words mean the same thing, so you can swap one into a sentence to check. If it still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: A sled is a small vehicle that slides over snow, used by children for fun on hills.
fact: In the UK this is called a sledge; in Australia people sometimes say toboggan.
fact: A bigger sled can be pulled by dogs or reindeer over snow and ice.
fact: Sled is also a verb: «We went sledding» means we rode a sled.
fact: Safety tip: «Sled feet first, sitting up, on a hill away from roads, trees and ponds.»
exercise: «Stop sledding when it gets dark, because you cannot see obstacles.» What does sledding mean here? | *Riding a sled down a hill | A pile of soft snow | A dark tunnel under the ground | A kind of winter sandwich
exercise: «Sled feet first, sitting up, on a hill away from roads, trees and ponds; head-first sledding causes injuries.» Why should you sled feet first? | *To avoid injuries | To make the sled turn left | Because sleds only work that way for dogs | To go faster than your friends
exercise: A bigger sled is pulled over snow and ice. What pulls it? | Ice cream | A bus full of children | *Dogs or reindeer | The wind on its own
test: What is the UK word for the same small vehicle? | Shovel | Snowman | Skate | *Sledge
test: «Head-first sledding causes injuries.» What does this sentence tell you? | *Head-first sledding is dangerous and can hurt you | Head-first sledding is only for grown-ups | Head-first sledding is the fastest way down | Head-first sledding is the best way to have fun
exam: Which sentence uses sled as a noun, meaning a thing? | We went sledding after lunch | Sled away from the trees and ponds | *We pulled our sled up the hill | Sled feet first, sitting up
